an is we had a rule set  that basically was written to allow  traffic and then left space in the  middle that specifically denies  particular traffic from particular  sources and then the default rule is  allow so in this way we've given ourself  room to create deny rules but we aren't  going to stumble and accidentally  blacklist one of our own addresses and  cause big problems one of the  interesting pieces about this because  cloud armor was in alpha we had a few  folks that were a little concerned at  target about how this would operate  would its scale and our Google partners  mentioned to us that this is used at the  Google edge for a lot of their products  and so that helped out but there was  still a little bit of concern and so  what we did  is we actually applied this policy to  all of our endpoints and we didn't tell  any of our application teams that we did  that and no one noticed a single thing  and everything went off without a hitch  so that was the kind of proof in the  pudding for us that cloud armor really  does work when we talk about how we're  using cloud armor today we're shifting  to a tactical whitelisting approach so  by that what I mean is we have several  rules in place that allow all of the  authorized traffic that I mentioned  earlier traffic from our CD ends and  data centers and things like that and  then right down at the bottom we deny  everything else so in doing this we  really restrict that to those approved  endpoints and through the architecture  that we've so carefully set up with  laughs and caching and all those great  things at the front end so we have these  security policies deployed to almost all  of our load balancers today and we have  just a few that we need to flip from  preview mode to enable mode and block  that traffic so just another tip of the  hat so the preview mode feature of cloud  armor it's worked really great and it's  helped our application teams identify in  some cases traffic from some of our  systems 